# Archetype: Drifting Goals (Senge — also "Eroding Goals")
#
# Structure: when actual performance falls short of the goal, the system
# can react in two ways:
#   B1: improve performance (real corrective action).
#   B2: lower the goal (redefine "good enough" downward).
# When B2 dominates — because lowering goals is cheaper or politically
# easier than improving — both performance AND aspirations spiral down.
#
# Real-world examples:
#   - Public service quality metrics gradually relaxed.
#   - Personal fitness goals walked back over years.
#   - Software response-time SLOs widened after each missed quarter.

title Drifting Goals - eroding service standard

StartTime = 0
EndTime = 80
TimeStep = 0.5

# What we actually deliver.
stock Performance = 100

# What we aim for.
stock Goal = 100

# How the gap is split between fixing performance vs lowering the goal.
# 0 = pure improvement, 1 = pure goal erosion.
constant GoalErosionPreference = 0.7

# How aggressively we react at all (per gap unit per unit time).
constant ReactionStrength = 0.05

# Continuous external pressure pulling performance down.
constant DownwardPressure = 1.5

# ─── B1: gap → improve Performance (corrective) ─────────────────────────
calc Gap = Goal - Performance
calc PerformanceImprovement = Gap * ReactionStrength * (1 - GoalErosionPreference)

flow Improve:
    PerformanceImprovement -+> Performance

# Continuous external pressure (entropy, demand growth, equipment ageing…).
flow Decay:
    DownwardPressure --> Performance

# ─── B2: gap → lower Goal (erosion) ─────────────────────────────────────
calc GoalErosion = Gap * ReactionStrength * GoalErosionPreference

flow LowerGoal:
    GoalErosion --> Goal

limit Performance min = 0
limit Goal min = 0

scenario AspireHigher:
    constant GoalErosionPreference = 0.1   # discipline: nearly all gap → improvement

scenario LetItDrift:
    constant GoalErosionPreference = 0.95  # rationalisation: nearly all gap → goal cut

plot Performance
plot Goal
plot Gap
